updated_at":"2017-10-21T20:57:54Z","closed_at":"2015-08-31T20:00:47Z","author_association":"NONE","type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"For equal or almost equal vectors (when `cosTheta` is ~= 1.0f) `glm::rotation()` can produce slightly incorrect quaternions. You can easily test it with this code:\n\n``` c++\nglm::vec3 testVec = glm::normalize(glm::vec3(5.0f, 5.0f, 5.0f));\nglm::quat rotQuat = glm::rotation(testVec, testVec); // Result will be (0.0f, 0.0f, 0.0f, 0.99999994f)\n```\n\nIt is OK in most cases, but there are specific exceptions. I think it would be better to add check to `glm::rotation()` for such cases, something like that:\n\n``` c++\nif (cosTheta >= static_cast<T>(1) - epsilon<T>())\n    return quat();\n```\n\nThis would also prevent unnecessary extra calculations like `glm::cross()` (which returns zero vector for that case).\n","closed_by":{"login":"Groovounet","id":367227,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM2NzIyNw==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/367227?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Groovounet","html_url":"https://github.com/Groovounet","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Groovounet/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Groovounet/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Groovounet/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Groovounet/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Groovounet/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Groovounet/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Groovounet/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Groovounet/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Groovounet/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"